<%- include('partials/header', {title: 'Highly Available WordPress on AWS'}) %>

<section class="project-hero">
  <a href="/" class="project-back-btn"><i class="fas fa-arrow-left"></i> Back to Portfolio</a>
  <h1>Highly Available WordPress on AWS</h1>
  <p>Production-grade WordPress with Auto Scaling, ALB, Aurora MySQL, EFS, CloudFront, and ElastiCache — focused on real-world debugging and architectural decision-making.</p>
  <div class="project-hero-tags">
    <span>Auto Scaling</span><span>ALB</span><span>Aurora MySQL</span><span>EFS</span><span>CloudFront</span><span>ElastiCache</span><span>VPC</span>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="project-details">
  <div class="center">
    <img src="/images/wordpress.png" alt="Highly Available WordPress Architecture" class="project-diagram">
  </div>

  <div class="project-section">
    <h3>Project Overview</h3>
    <p>This project demonstrates designing and validating a <strong>Highly Available WordPress architecture</strong> on AWS, with a focus on <strong>troubleshooting production issues</strong> rather than just deployment — including ALB health check failures, Auto Scaling tuning, and end-to-end validation.</p>
    <div class="highlight-box">
      <strong>Key Focus Areas</strong>
      <ul>
        <li>Real-world debugging and root-cause analysis</li>
        <li>Performance optimization and bottleneck identification</li>
        <li>Architectural decision-making for production workloads</li>
        <li>End-to-end flow validation and monitoring</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="project-section alt-bg">
    <h3>Architecture Components</h3>
    <div class="architecture-grid">
      <div class="arch-component">
        <h4>🌐 Network Layer</h4>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Custom VPC</strong> with public/private subnets</li>
          <li><strong>Internet Gateway</strong> for public access</li>
          <li><strong>NAT Gateway</strong> for private subnet outbound</li>
          <li><strong>NACLs</strong> for subnet-level security</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="arch-component">
        <h4>⚖️ Load Balancing & Scaling</h4>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Application Load Balancer</strong> with target groups</li>
          <li><strong>Auto Scaling Group</strong> with tuned grace periods</li>
          <li><strong>Health checks</strong> for instance monitoring</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="arch-component">
        <h4>💾 Data Layer</h4>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Aurora MySQL</strong> in private subnets</li>
          <li><strong>Amazon EFS</strong> for shared WordPress files</li>
          <li><strong>ElastiCache (Redis)</strong> for caching</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="arch-component">
        <h4>🚀 Performance & Delivery</h4>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>CloudFront CDN</strong> for low-latency delivery</li>
          <li><strong>Connection pooling</strong> and optimization</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="project-section">
    <h3>Real-World Troubleshooting</h3>
    <div class="troubleshooting-grid">
      <div class="trouble-item">
        <h4>🎯 ALB Health Check Failures</h4>
        <p>Debugged target group health check failures, identified root causes, and implemented proper health check endpoints.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="trouble-item">
        <h4>📈 Auto Scaling Optimization</h4>
        <p>Tuned health check grace periods and scaling policies to prevent premature termination.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="trouble-item">
        <h4>🔍 Backend Bottleneck Analysis</h4>
        <p>Traced performance limits to the backend instead of adding more EC2 instances — focused on database and caching improvements.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="trouble-item">
        <h4>🌊 End-to-End Flow Validation</h4>
        <p>Validated the full request path: CloudFront → ALB → EC2 → Aurora, confirming each component worked correctly.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="project-section alt-bg">
    <h3>Notes & Best Practices</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Always place <strong>Aurora and backend servers</strong> in private subnets.</li>
      <li>Use <strong>EFS for shared WordPress files</strong> across multiple EC2 instances.</li>
      <li>Tune <strong>Auto Scaling grace periods</strong> to allow WordPress to fully boot before health checks.</li>
      <li>Monitor the <strong>full request path</strong> — don't scale blindly, find the actual bottleneck first.</li>
      <li>Use <strong>ElastiCache</strong> to reduce database load for repeated queries.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="center">
    <a href="/" class="btn-back"><i class="fas fa-arrow-left"></i> Back to Portfolio</a>
  </div>
</div>

<%- include('partials/footer') %>
